= 3. Traditional Security & Arms Cooperation: Disarmament & Control

Even within the traditional military security paradigm, sovereign states recognize that unrestrained arms races generate ruinous financial burdens and increase the risk of accidental mutual annihilation. Consequently, states cooperate through treaty frameworks:

== Disarmament Regimes: Complete Weapon Elimination
- *Biological Weapons Convention (BWC, 1972):* Formally known as the Convention on the Prohibition of the Development, Production and Stockpiling of Bacteriological (Biological) and Toxin Weapons. Signed in 1972 and entered into force in 1975, it comprehensively banned an entire category of weapons of mass destruction, ratified by over 180 countries.
- *Chemical Weapons Convention (CWC, 1992):* Concluded in Geneva in 1992 and opened for signature in Paris in 1993, the CWC outlawed the development, production, acquisition, stockpiling, transfer, and use of chemical weapons. It mandated the verifiably supervised destruction of all chemical arsenals under the *Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (OPCW)*, based in The Hague.

== Arms Control Regimes: Regulating and Limiting Arsenals
- *Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty (NPT, 1968):*
  - Designed to halt the horizontal spread of nuclear weapons capability.
  - *The Discriminatory Cutoff:* Article IX defined a nuclear-weapon state exclusively as one that had manufactured and exploded a nuclear device prior to *1 January 1967* (the US, USSR, UK, France, and China).
  - *India's Principled Stance:* India rejected the NPT, characterizing it as institutionalized nuclear apartheid that granted exclusive legal legitimacy to the arsenals of five states while permanently denying defensive deterrents to the rest of humanity.
- *Anti-Ballistic Missile (ABM) Treaty (1972):* Signed between the United States and the Soviet Union, restricting the deployment of nationwide missile defense shields to preserve mutual vulnerability and maintain nuclear deterrence stability. (The US unilaterally abrogated the treaty in 2002).
- *Strategic Arms Limitation Talks (SALT I & II):* Superpower bilateral agreements freezing intercontinental ballistic missile (ICBM) and submarine-launched ballistic missile (SLBM) launchers in the 1970s.
- *Strategic Arms Reduction Treaties (START I & II):* Historic bilateral pacts signed in the 1990s mandating deep, verifiable dismantling of deployed strategic nuclear warheads.

== Confidence-Building Measures (CBMs)
- Institutional mechanisms designed to prevent accidental war:
  - Exchanging military data and strategic force deployment details.
  - Establishing emergency communication "hotlines" between military commands.
  - Providing advance formal notification of major military troop maneuvers.

